.TH QTOP 1 "2026-11-23" "qtop 0.9.20261123" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
qtop \- text-mode monitor of batch-cluster utilization and job status
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qtop
.RB [ \-FGT ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B qtop \-w
.RI [ seconds ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B qtop \-R
.I datetime
.RI [ duration ]
.br
.B qtop \-\-load\-sample
.I archive
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qtop
queries a batch scheduler (PBS/Torque, SGE, Slurm or OAR) and prints four
numbered sections on one screen:
.TP
.B 1. Job Accounting Summary
running and queued jobs per queue or partition, and node and core totals.
.TP
.B 2. Worker Node Occupancy
a matrix with one column per worker node and one row per core; each cell
shows the user (by an id letter and color) whose job occupies that core.
.TP
.B 3. User Accounts and Pool Mappings
the per-user legend for the matrix, with job counts by state.
.TP
.B 4. Account totals
independent per-state job and node totals across users.
.PP
Without
.BR \-w ,
qtop prints once and exits. Scheduler output is saved under
.I savepath
(see
.BR FILES ),
so a view can be replayed later with
.BR \-R .
The
.B demo
scheduler generates a synthetic cluster and needs no scheduler commands.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Scheduler and input
.TP
.BI \-b " name" "\fR, \fP" \-\-batchSystem " name"
Scheduler to query:
.BR pbs ,
.BR sge ,
.BR slurm ,
.BR slurm_json ,
.BR oar ,
.BR lsf ,
.BR htcondor ,
.B demo
or
.B auto
(detect from the commands found in
.BR PATH ).
.TP
.BI \-s " path" "\fR, \fP" \-\-SetSourceDir " path"
Read scheduler traces from a directory or a tar archive instead of running
scheduler commands; a plain output file selects its parent directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-load\-sample " archive"
Reproduce an anonymized support snapshot (made with
.BR "\-L \-A" )
without scheduler commands.
.TP
.B \-\-slurm-summary
Collect only sinfo and squeue on each display cycle, with the legacy slurm
backend; see
.BR "SLURM SUMMARY COLLECTION" .
.TP
.B \-\-slurm-json-squeue
Use squeue \-\-json \-\-states=all for slurm_json jobs instead of
scontrol JSON, for clients returning empty job responses.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Include hidden and group-unavailable partitions in live Slurm queries
(subject to permissions).
.TP
.BR \-G ", " \-\-get_GECOS_via_getent_passwd
Look up user details with
.B getent passwd
for every user in the scheduler output.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-StrictCheck
Compare the running-job total reported by the scheduler with the jobs qtop
found and displayed.
.SS Display
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-disablesection1
Hide the Job Accounting Summary.
.TP
.BR \-2 ", " \-\-disablesection2
Hide the Worker Node Occupancy matrix.
.TP
.BR \-3 ", " \-\-disablesection3
Hide the User Accounts and Pool Mappings.
.TP
.BR \-4 ", " \-\-noaccounttotals
Hide independent section 4, Account totals, shown by default.
.TP
.B \-\-accounttotals
Show Account totals independently of section 3 (the default; up to 0.9.20261110,
.B \-4
meant this).
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-COLOR " " \fBON\fP | \fBOFF\fP | \fBAUTO\fP
Color output.
.B AUTO
(the default) uses color on a terminal; see
.B QTOP_COLOR
in
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-classic
Mimic the legacy qtop display as far as possible.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-wrap\-queues
Keep the Queueing summary on one physical terminal row, clipping overflow.
Mutually exclusive with --expand-queues. Q cycles compact, expanded, clipped and
hidden layouts in watch mode; hidden drops the Queueing line. Clipped overflow is the default.
The blocked-queue legend stays visible on its own line.
Normalized exports retain every queue.
.TP
.BR \-\-expand\-queues
Show one queue or partition per line in the accounting summary (cycle layouts with
.B Q
in watch mode).
.TP
.BR \-\-show\-groups
Show each active user's primary Unix group in the account table.
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-ForceNames
Show node names instead of numbered worker nodes, even for small clusters.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-blindremapping
Number worker nodes in order of appearance; for node names that are not a
plain arithmetic sequence (for example Rocks clusters).
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-noMasking
Do not hide leading empty worker nodes (by default, if the first 30 nodes
are unused, numbering starts at 31).
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-removeemptycorelines
Remove matrix rows consisting only of non-existent cores
.RB ( # ).
Given twice
.RB ( \-rr ),
also remove rows of free cores
.RB ( _ ).
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-Transpose
Rotate the matrix by 90 degrees.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-less
Let the matrix overflow the terminal width, for piping into
.BR "less \-RS" .
.SS Run modes
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-watch " [\fIseconds\fP]"
Refresh like
.BR watch (1)
every
.I seconds
(default 2), with interactive navigation (see
.BR "INTERACTIVE COMMANDS" ).
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-replay " \fIdatetime\fP [\fIduration\fP]"
Replay saved output from
.IR datetime ,
given as
.I yyyymmddTHHMMSS
(e.g. 20161118T182300),
.I HHMM
(today) or
.I mmddTHHMM
(this year).
.I duration
is
.IR N h,
.IR N m
or
.IR N s
(default 2m).
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-\-onlysavetofile
Save results without printing them.
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-export
Export cluster data as JSON.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-sample
Create a support archive with the log, the scheduler output and the qtop
output. With
.BR \-A ,
store a portable masked snapshot instead. Given twice
.RB ( \-LL ),
also add package sources and runtime assets and, without
.BR \-A ,
the bundled defaults and effective configuration.
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-anonymize
Mask identifiers where the backend supports it.
.TP
.B \-\-raw\-companion
With
.BR "\-L \-A \-e" ,
also save a separate, private archive of the unmasked scheduler input.
.TP
.B \-\-masked\-traces
With
.BR "\-L \-A \-e" ,
add experimental masked scheduler-format traces; unsupported files are
omitted.
.SS Configuration
.TP
.BI \-f " file" "\fR, \fP" \-\-setCUSTOMCONFFILE " file"
Use
.I file
as the custom configuration (see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.TP
.BI \-o " key" = value "\fR, \fP" \-\-option " key" = value
Override a top-level configuration key; may be repeated. Values are parsed as
Python literals where possible and are never executed.
.SS Experimental
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-experimental
Required by the experimental features below.
.TP
.B \-\-queue\-nodes
Show idle and total node counts per queue (requires
.BR \-e ).
.TP
.BR \-B ", " \-\-web
Serve the view on
.I http://127.0.0.1:8080/
(requires
.BR \-e ).
Only
.IR / ,
.I /index.html
and
.I /api.json
are served. There is no login or TLS; change the address with
.B experimental_web_host
and
.B experimental_web_port
only behind an authenticating reverse proxy.
.SS Diagnostics
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Print debug messages and operational-error tracebacks to standard error and
the log file.
.TP
.B \-\-demanding
Turn on every strict check at once:
.BR \-S ,
.BR \-\-strict\-parents ,
and validating each live-collected cluster state a second time when the
snapshot is built (live collection otherwise validates it once). Slower on
large clusters.
.TP
.B \-\-strict\-parents
Stop when a parent directory of
.I savepath
or of the log file is writable by group or others without the sticky bit.
By default qtop continues and notes such a parent at
.B \-v
verbosity; shared site trees such as a group-writable
.I /home
or
.I /gpfs/home
are common. The directories qtop owns are always created or tightened to mode
0700 and never followed through symbolic links. It also stops on an unusable
.I savepath
or log instead of warning and falling back to a private
.I qtop_fallback_<uid>
directory under
.IR /dev/shm ,
the temporary directory or
.BR QTOP_FALLBACK_DIR .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Increase verbosity; may be repeated.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH INTERACTIVE COMMANDS
In watch mode
.RB ( \-w ):
.TP 16
.BR j " or " Space
down one screen
.TQ
.BR k " or " Backspace
up one screen
.TQ
.BR h " / " l
left / right one screen
.TQ
.BR g " or " K
top of the matrix
.TQ
.BR G " or " J
bottom of the matrix
.TQ
.BR 0 " / " $
left / right edge
.TQ
.B R
reset the viewport
.TQ
.B f
filter
.TQ
.B F
switch full names / numbered node labels
.TQ
.B H
highlight users or queues
.TQ
.B m
color cores by user / queue
.TQ
.B p / P
cycle the colour palettes forward / backward
.TQ
.B Q
cycle compact / expanded / clipped / hidden queue layouts
.TQ
.B r
cycle all / real / used core lines
.TQ
.B s
sort
.TQ
.B S
stack each node's jobs from its first core / show scheduler core placement
.TQ
.B t
transpose the matrix
.TQ
.B 1 2 3
show / hide sections 1-3
.TQ
.B 4
show / hide independent section 4, Account totals
.TQ
.B ?
toggle help
.TQ
.B q
quit
.PP
Arrow Up/Down moves one displayed row; Left/Right moves horizontally.
Home or g goes to the first line; End or G goes to the last page.
Bare 1 toggles section 1 immediately; G is a separate key.
PageUp/PageDown moves one page. /text followed by Enter searches literal text,
wrapping at the end; an empty search repeats. :42 followed by Enter jumps to
displayed row 42. Escape cancels, Backspace edits, and Ctrl-U clears a prompt.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are read in this order, later ones replacing earlier ones:
.IP 1. 4
the bundled
.I qtopconf.yaml
.IP 2. 4
.I /etc/qtopconf.yaml
.IP 3. 4
.I $HOME/.local/qtop/qtopconf.yaml
.IP 4. 4
the file given with
.BR \-f ;
a relative name is looked up in
.I $HOME/.local/qtop
and then in the current directory
.IP 5. 4
each
.B \-o
override, from left to right
.PP
A file replaces whole top-level values; nested mappings such as
.B schedulers
are not merged. The scheduler is chosen by
.BR \-b ,
then
.BR QTOP_SCHEDULER ,
then the
.B scheduler
key.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B QTOP_SCHEDULER
Scheduler to use when
.B \-b
is not given.
.TP
.B QTOP_COLOR
With
.BR "\-c AUTO" :
.B ON
forces color; any other value disables it; when unset, color is used on a
terminal.
.TP
.B QTOP_FALLBACK_DIR
Directory for the best-effort
.I qtop_fallback_<uid>
used when
.I savepath
or the log is unusable; by default
.I /dev/shm
then the temporary directory.
.TP
.BR HOME ", " USER
Locate the per-user configuration and log, and expand the default
.IR savepath .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/qtopconf.yaml
site configuration
.TP
.I $HOME/.local/qtop/qtopconf.yaml
user configuration
.TP
.I $HOME/.local/qtop/logs/qtop.log
log file (mode 0600)
.TP
.I /tmp/qtop_results_$USER
default
.IR savepath :
saved scheduler output and replay data, created with mode 0700
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success
.TP
.B 1
no usable scheduler, or an operational error (failed or timed-out scheduler
command, unreadable input, invalid configuration)
.TP
.B 2
invalid command-line arguments
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B qtop \-b demo
Show a synthetic cluster.
.TP
.B qtop \-w 10
Refresh every 10 seconds, auto-detecting the scheduler.
.TP
.B qtop \-b slurm \-c OFF \-1 \-3
Print only the occupancy matrix, without color.
.TP
.B qtop \-R 1823 1h
Replay one hour starting today at 18:23.
.TP
.B qtop \-e \-A \-L
Create a masked support archive.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR squeue (1),
.BR sinfo (1),
.BR qstat (1),
.BR pbsnodes (8),
.BR oarstat (1),
.BR watch (1)
.PP
Full documentation:
.I https://github.com/qtop/qtop/blob/main/docs/documentation.md
